//! check: run(exit=3)
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.process
//!
//! `process.exit` ends THIS process with the status the caller names, and
//! the record's verdict is the witness: `exit(3)`, not a trap and not a
//! propagated row. Nothing after the call runs, which is why the line that
//! would print sits after it.
//!
//! The low-byte rule (`exit(300)` is seen as 44) is documented on the
//! function and deliberately NOT tested here: a second entry file per
//! platform-visible arithmetic would be witnessing the host's `waitpid`
//! encoding rather than std's behaviour, and this rig observes the verdict
//! through the same encoding.

use std.process

fn main() -> !int {
    process.exit(3)
    print("unreachable")
    0
}
